window.RUST_QUESTIONS = {"rust-at-a-glance":{"topic":"rust-at-a-glance","title":"Rust at a glance","quiz":[{"q":"What are the two main things Rust is designed to give you at the same time?","options":["Speed and memory safety","A garbage collector and dynamic typing","Short code and no compiler","Automatic parallelism and a REPL"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rust's whole pitch is programs that are both fast and free of memory bugs, achieved without a garbage collector."},{"q":"How does Rust manage memory safety?","options":["A background garbage collector cleans up while the program runs","You call free() by hand like in C","The compiler checks ownership rules before the program runs and rejects unsafe code","It never frees memory, so programs leak"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust has no garbage collector and no manual free(). The compiler enforces ownership rules at compile time, refusing to build unsafe code."},{"q":"Which single concept is described as the core idea that makes the rest of Rust click?","options":["Macros","Ownership","Async/await","Unsafe blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ownership is Rust's rule for who is responsible for each value and when it's cleaned up. It's the heart of the language."},{"q":"For a beginner, which topics should you save for later rather than start with?","options":["Variables and functions","Printing and if/else","Macros, unsafe, and async","Reading compiler errors"],"answer":2,"explain":"Macros, unsafe, and async are advanced. Beginners should orient first, then learn basics and ownership before touching them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What two qualities does Rust aim to deliver together?","back":"Speed (like C/C++) and memory safety (like garbage-collected languages) — with no garbage collector."},{"front":"When does Rust check that your memory use is safe?","back":"At compile time. The compiler enforces ownership rules before the program ever runs and refuses to build unsafe code."},{"front":"What does 'Rust is explicit by default' mean?","back":"Rust rarely does hidden things behind your back; you say what you mean, so code tells you the truth about what it does."},{"front":"What is the one big idea to learn slowly, because it makes the rest of Rust make sense?","back":"Ownership — who owns each value and when it gets cleaned up."}]},"install-rust":{"topic":"install-rust","title":"Install Rust","quiz":[{"q":"What is the official, recommended way to install Rust?","options":["rustup, from rustup.rs","Your Linux distro's package manager","Downloading rustc.exe from a random site","Homebrew only"],"answer":0,"explain":"rustup (from rustup.rs) is the official installer. It installs and updates rustc and cargo and can switch toolchains."},{"q":"You just installed Rust but your terminal says 'command not found' for cargo. What's the most likely fix?","options":["Reinstall your operating system","Open a new terminal so the PATH change takes effect","Delete rustup and use apt instead","Nothing, Rust is broken"],"answer":1,"explain":"An already-open terminal doesn't see the new PATH. Opening a fresh terminal (or sourcing ~/.cargo/env) usually fixes it."},{"q":"Which command confirms the compiler is installed and reachable?","options":["rust --check","cargo install","rustc --version","rustup remove"],"answer":2,"explain":"rustc --version prints the compiler's version number, confirming it's installed and on your PATH."},{"q":"How do you update to the latest stable Rust after it's installed?","options":["rustup update","cargo new update","rustc --upgrade","You must reinstall from scratch each time"],"answer":0,"explain":"rustup update fetches the latest stable rustc and cargo and swaps them in. Rust releases roughly every six weeks."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What tool installs and updates Rust, and where do you get it?","back":"rustup, from rustup.rs. It manages rustc and cargo and keeps them updated."},{"front":"After installing Rust, why might 'cargo' still be 'command not found'?","back":"Your open terminal hasn't picked up the new PATH. Open a new terminal (or run: source \"$HOME/.cargo/env\")."},{"front":"How do you verify the install worked?","back":"Run rustc --version and cargo --version — both should print a version number."},{"front":"How do you update Rust to the latest stable version?","back":"rustup update"}]},"cargo-basics":{"topic":"cargo-basics","title":"Cargo basics","quiz":[{"q":"What does `cargo run` do?","options":["Only downloads dependencies","Builds your code and then runs the resulting program","Deletes the target folder","Uploads your crate to crates.io"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo run compiles your project and immediately runs the built program — two steps in one command."},{"q":"Which file lists your project's dependencies?","options":["src/main.rs","Cargo.lock","Cargo.toml","target/debug"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cargo.toml is the project manifest; dependencies go under its [dependencies] section (cargo add edits it for you)."},{"q":"You want to quickly check that your code compiles, without producing a finished program. Which command is fastest for that?","options":["cargo check","cargo build --release","cargo clean","cargo new"],"answer":0,"explain":"cargo check verifies the code compiles without generating the final executable, so it's faster — ideal while fixing errors."},{"q":"What is the purpose of Cargo.lock?","options":["It stores your source code","It records the exact dependency versions so builds are repeatable","It locks the file so you can't edit main.rs","It is a password file for crates.io"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo.lock pins the exact versions of every dependency, so the project builds identically on every machine. Commit it for applications."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which single command builds and runs a Rust project?","back":"cargo run"},{"front":"Where do a project's dependencies get listed?","back":"In Cargo.toml, under [dependencies]. You can add them with `cargo add <crate>`."},{"front":"What does `cargo check` do differently from `cargo build`?","back":"It verifies the code compiles without producing the final executable, so it's faster — great while fixing errors."},{"front":"What is Cargo.lock for, and should you commit it?","back":"It records exact dependency versions for repeatable builds. Commit it for applications."}]},"hello-world":{"topic":"hello-world","title":"Hello, world","quiz":[{"q":"Where does a Rust program start running?","options":["At the first line of the file","At the fn main() function","At whichever function is alphabetically first","Wherever you put println!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Execution always begins at fn main() — it's the entry point, the 'front door' of the program."},{"q":"Why does println! have an exclamation mark?","options":["It makes the text louder","It marks the end of the line","Because println! is a macro, not a regular function","It is optional decoration"],"answer":2,"explain":"The ! tells you it's a macro. If you drop it, the compiler can't find a function named 'println' and errors out."},{"q":"What does {} do inside println!(\"Day {}\", day)?","options":["It prints literal curly braces","It is a placeholder that gets filled with the value of `day`","It starts a new code block","Nothing, it is ignored"],"answer":1,"explain":"{} is a placeholder. Rust substitutes the matching value (here, `day`) into that spot."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the entry point of every Rust executable?","back":"fn main() — the program always starts there."},{"front":"What does the ! in println! mean?","back":"It means println! is a macro (not a normal function). Macros are called with a ! after the name."},{"front":"How do you print the value of a variable x inside text?","back":"Use a {} placeholder: println!(\"value is {}\", x);  or name it inline: println!(\"value is {x}\");"}]},"reading-errors":{"topic":"reading-errors","title":"Reading compiler errors","quiz":[{"q":"When several errors appear at once, which should you usually fix first?","options":["The last one in the list","The first one, then rebuild","The one with the longest message","A random one, they're all independent"],"answer":1,"explain":"One early mistake often causes a cascade of later errors. Fix the first, rebuild, and many others often disappear."},{"q":"In an error message, what does the `--> src/main.rs:4:20` line tell you?","options":["The number of errors found","The file, line, and column where the problem is","The version of the compiler","How long the build took"],"answer":1,"explain":"The --> line is the location: file src/main.rs, line 4, column 20 — where the compiler wants you to look first."},{"q":"What's the difference between a `warning` and an `error`?","options":["Warnings stop the build; errors don't","They are exactly the same thing","A warning still compiles and runs; an error stops the build","Errors are only shown in release mode"],"answer":2,"explain":"A warning compiles and runs but flags something suspicious. Only an error blocks the build."},{"q":"You see a line starting with `help:` suggesting a change. What should you do?","options":["Ignore it and guess your own fix","Try the suggested fix first — it's usually correct","Delete the whole function","Run cargo clean"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's help: suggestions are right most of the time. Try the suggestion before guessing at other changes."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When you get many compiler errors, which one do you fix first?","back":"The first one, then rebuild — later errors are often just fallout from the first."},{"front":"What does the `-->` line in a Rust error tell you?","back":"The location of the problem: file, line, and column (e.g. src/main.rs:4:20)."},{"front":"Does a `warning` stop your program from building?","back":"No. A warning still compiles and runs; only an `error` blocks the build."},{"front":"How do you get a longer explanation of an error code like E0382?","back":"Run `rustc --explain E0382`, or check the online error index."}]},"variables-and-mutability":{"topic":"variables-and-mutability","title":"Variables and mutability","quiz":[{"q":"By default, what happens if you try to reassign a variable created with `let`?","options":["It works fine; variables are mutable by default","The compiler errors: cannot assign twice to an immutable variable","It silently creates a new variable","It only fails if the type changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variables are immutable by default. Reassigning without `mut` gives 'cannot assign twice to immutable variable'."},{"q":"What is the difference between `mut` and shadowing?","options":["They are two names for the same thing","mut changes a value in place (same type); shadowing makes a new variable that can change type","Shadowing is faster than mut","mut only works on numbers, shadowing only on text"],"answer":1,"explain":"`mut` reassigns the same variable and the type must stay the same. Shadowing (`let x` again) creates a brand-new variable, which may even have a different type."},{"q":"What does `let x = 5; let x = x + 1;` do?","options":["Errors because x is immutable","Changes the original x to 6","Creates a new variable x (shadowing) whose value is 6; the old one is hidden","Creates two variables both visible at once"],"answer":2,"explain":"The second `let x` shadows the first — a new variable reusing the name. The old x is hidden, not mutated."},{"q":"You wrote `let mut n = 3;` but never change `n`. What does Rust do?","options":["Nothing, it's perfectly fine","Refuses to compile","Warns that the variable does not need to be mutable","Automatically removes the mut"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust warns 'variable does not need to be mutable' — a hint that your `mut` and your actual usage disagree."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you make a variable changeable?","back":"Add `mut`: `let mut x = 5;`. Without it, variables are immutable by default."},{"front":"What is shadowing?","back":"Writing `let` again with the same name. It creates a NEW variable (the old one is hidden) and it may have a different type."},{"front":"mut vs shadowing — the one-line difference","back":"mut = same variable changes in place, type stays the same. Shadowing = new variable reusing the name, type can change."},{"front":"What is a `const` in Rust?","back":"A value fixed at compile time, always UPPER_SNAKE_CASE with an explicit type, e.g. `const MAX: u32 = 3;`. Never uses mut."}]},"data-types":{"topic":"data-types","title":"Data types","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `u` in `u32` mean?","options":["Universal — works with any value","Unsigned — zero or positive only, no negatives","Unlimited size","Unicode text"],"answer":1,"explain":"`u` means unsigned (0 or positive). `i` means signed (can be negative). The 32 is the number of bits."},{"q":"Which default type does Rust pick for `let x = 5;`?","options":["u8","f64","i32","usize"],"answer":2,"explain":"With no annotation, an integer literal defaults to `i32`. A decimal literal like 3.14 defaults to `f64`."},{"q":"What is the difference between a tuple and an array?","options":["Tuples can hold different types; arrays hold one type, all the same","Arrays can hold different types; tuples hold one type","They are identical","Tuples can grow; arrays cannot"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tuple groups a fixed number of possibly-different types. An array holds many values of the same type at a fixed length."},{"q":"How do you write a single character value like the letter A?","options":["\"A\" with double quotes","'A' with single quotes","char(A)","`A`"],"answer":1,"explain":"A `char` uses single quotes: 'A'. Double quotes \"A\" make text (a string slice), which is a different type."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Decode the type name `i32`.","back":"`i` = signed (can be negative), `32` = 32 bits. It's the everyday default integer type."},{"front":"Array vs tuple?","back":"Array: many values of the SAME type, fixed length, indexed with [ ]. Tuple: fixed number of possibly-DIFFERENT types, accessed with .0, .1."},{"front":"Single quotes vs double quotes?","back":"Single quotes 'A' = one char. Double quotes \"A\" = text (a string). Different types."},{"front":"I need a list that grows. Array or Vec?","back":"A Vec. Arrays are a fixed length that can't grow or shrink; a growing collection is a Vec."}]},"functions":{"topic":"functions","title":"Functions","quiz":[{"q":"In a function body, how do you return the last expression's value?","options":["Write it on the last line WITH a semicolon","Write it on the last line WITHOUT a semicolon","You must always use the `return` keyword","Assign it to a variable called `result`"],"answer":1,"explain":"The final expression, written without a trailing semicolon, is the function's return value. No `return` keyword needed."},{"q":"What happens if you add a semicolon after `a + b` in a function that returns i32?","options":["Nothing, it works the same","It returns a + b twice","It throws the value away and returns () — a 'mismatched types' error","It rounds the result"],"answer":2,"explain":"The semicolon turns the expression into a statement, discarding the value. The function then returns () (nothing), causing 'expected i32, found ()'."},{"q":"Which is a correctly written function signature?","options":["fn add(a, b) { a + b }","fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }","fn add(a: i32, b: i32) { a + b }","fn add -> i32 (a, b) { a + b }"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every parameter needs a type, and a function that returns a value must declare it with `-> Type`. Rust never infers parameter types."},{"q":"When is the `return` keyword actually needed?","options":["Always, on every return","Never; Rust forbids it","To return early, before the end of the function","Only inside loops"],"answer":2,"explain":"The last expression returns implicitly. You use `return` when you want to leave the function early, before reaching the end."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does a Rust function return a value without `return`?","back":"The last expression in the body, written with NO semicolon, is the return value."},{"front":"What does a trailing semicolon do to a return value?","back":"It throws the value away — the expression becomes a statement returning () (nothing). Very common bug."},{"front":"What must every function parameter have?","back":"A type. `fn add(a: i32, b: i32)`. Rust never infers parameter types."},{"front":"How do you declare a function's return type?","back":"With an arrow: `fn name(...) -> ReturnType`. Leave the arrow off if the function returns nothing."}]},"control-flow":{"topic":"control-flow","title":"Control flow","quiz":[{"q":"What must an `if` condition be in Rust?","options":["Any non-zero number is true","A real bool (true or false)","A string that says \"true\"","Anything at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust requires an actual bool. It does not treat numbers as true/false, so `if count` fails — write `if count != 0`."},{"q":"What values does the range `1..4` produce in a for loop?","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","1, 2, 3","2, 3, 4","0, 1, 2, 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"`1..4` excludes the end, giving 1, 2, 3. To include 4, use the inclusive range `1..=4`."},{"q":"Which statement about `if` in Rust is true?","options":["if can only be a statement, never produce a value","if is an expression; you can write `let x = if c { 1 } else { 2 };`","if always returns the number 1 when true","if branches can each return a different type"],"answer":1,"explain":"`if` is an expression and can produce a value, but every branch must return the same type."},{"q":"Which loop is best for walking through each item of a collection?","options":["loop","while","for","if"],"answer":2,"explain":"`for item in collection` hands you each element directly and can't run off the end, making it the safest, most common choice."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Can you write `if count { ... }` where count is a number?","back":"No. Conditions must be a real bool. Write the comparison, e.g. `if count != 0 { ... }`."},{"front":"Difference between `1..4` and `1..=4`?","back":"`1..4` excludes the end → 1,2,3. `1..=4` includes it → 1,2,3,4."},{"front":"Name Rust's three loops and their use.","back":"loop = forever until break; while = while a condition holds; for = walk each item of a collection."},{"front":"Is `if` an expression in Rust?","back":"Yes — it can produce a value: `let x = if c { 1 } else { 2 };`. All branches must share one type."}]},"comments-and-documentation":{"topic":"comments-and-documentation","title":"Comments and documentation","quiz":[{"q":"Which comment style creates documentation that `cargo doc` turns into a website?","options":["// two slashes","/* block comment */","/// three slashes","# hash"],"answer":2,"explain":"`///` is a doc comment that describes the item below it and feeds `cargo doc`. `//` is just an ordinary human note."},{"q":"What is the best thing to explain in a comment?","options":["What each line does, restating the code","Why the code is there — the reasoning or a tricky edge case","The author's name on every line","The type of every variable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Good comments explain WHY, not WHAT. The code already shows what it does; the comment should add reasoning the code can't."},{"q":"Where must a `///` doc comment be placed?","options":["At the end of any line","Directly above the item it documents (a function, struct, etc.)","Anywhere, it doesn't matter","Only inside main"],"answer":1,"explain":"A `///` must sit directly above the item it describes. Floating with nothing below it is an error; use `//!` for inner (module/file) docs."},{"q":"What is special about code examples inside doc comments?","options":["They are ignored entirely","They are run as tests by `cargo test`, so they can't go stale","They slow the program down","They must be written in another language"],"answer":1,"explain":"Doc-comment examples are compiled and run as tests, which keeps the documentation honest and up to date."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"`//` vs `///`?","back":"`//` is an ordinary comment (a human note the compiler ignores). `///` is a doc comment that documents the item below it and feeds `cargo doc`."},{"front":"What should a good comment explain?","back":"The WHY (reasoning, tricky cases), not the WHAT. Don't restate what the code already shows."},{"front":"What does `//!` do?","back":"It documents the thing it's INSIDE (a whole module or file), rather than the item below it. Used at the top of files."},{"front":"What happens to code inside a doc-comment example?","back":"It's run as a test by `cargo test`, so examples stay correct."}]},"modules-and-crates":{"topic":"modules-and-crates","title":"Modules and crates","quiz":[{"q":"What is a crate in Rust?","options":["A single function","The unit Rust compiles — a package, either a runnable binary or a reusable library","A type of variable","A folder on your desktop"],"answer":1,"explain":"A crate is the compilation unit: a binary crate runs (has main), a library crate is used by other crates."},{"q":"By default, an item inside a module is...","options":["Public to everyone","Private — only visible inside its own module","Deleted at compile time","Automatically exported to the internet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Everything is private by default. You add `pub` to expose an item to code outside its module."},{"q":"How do you call a function `parse` from inside a module named `parser`?","options":["parser.parse()","parser->parse()","parser::parse()","parse.parser()"],"answer":2,"explain":"You reach into a module with the `::` path separator: `parser::parse()`. A dot is for method calls and field access."},{"q":"What does the `use` keyword do?","options":["Declares a new module","Brings a name into scope so you can type its short form","Deletes a module","Makes an item public"],"answer":1,"explain":"`use` imports a name so you can refer to it directly, e.g. `use parser::parse;` then just `parse(...)`. It does not create a module."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Binary crate vs library crate?","back":"Binary crate has `main` and runs. Library crate has no main; it offers functions/types for other crates to use."},{"front":"What's the default visibility of items in a module?","back":"Private — visible only inside their own module. Add `pub` to expose them."},{"front":"How do you reach into a module?","back":"With `::` (the path separator): `parser::parse()`. A dot is for methods/fields, not modules."},{"front":"`mod` vs `use`?","back":"`mod` declares a module (creates it or points at its file). `use` just shortens an existing name so you can type less."}]},"structs":{"topic":"structs","title":"Structs","quiz":[{"q":"What happens if you leave out a field when constructing a struct?","options":["The field defaults to zero","It compiles but the field is empty","A compile error: missing fields","The field is filled with the previous struct's value"],"answer":2,"explain":"You must provide every field in a struct literal (unless you copy the rest with struct update syntax). Missing one is a compile error."},{"q":"How do you make a single field of a struct mutable?","options":["Put `mut` before that field in the definition","You can't — mutability is on the binding: `let mut instance = ...` makes the whole struct mutable","Use `pub mut` on the field","Fields are always mutable"],"answer":1,"explain":"There is no per-field mut. `let mut user = ...` makes the entire struct changeable; without mut, no field can be assigned."},{"q":"How do you read the `name` field of a struct value `user`?","options":["user::name","user->name","user.name","name(user)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fields are accessed with a dot: `user.name`. The `::` is for paths and associated functions, not field access."},{"q":"What is the field init shorthand?","options":["Writing `name` instead of `name: name` when a variable shares the field's name","A way to skip fields entirely","Automatically generating a constructor","Making all fields public"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a variable has the same name as a field, you can write just `name` instead of `name: name`. Rust matches them up."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What must you provide when constructing a struct?","back":"Every field, by name: `User { id: 1, name: ..., active: true }`. Missing a field is a compile error."},{"front":"How do you make a struct's fields changeable?","back":"Make the binding mutable: `let mut user = ...`. There's no per-field mut; it's the whole struct or nothing."},{"front":"What is a tuple struct?","back":"A named type with positional (unnamed) fields: `struct Point(i32, i32);`. Access with .0, .1. Distinct type from a plain tuple."},{"front":"Field init shorthand?","back":"When a variable's name matches the field, write `name` instead of `name: name`."}]},"enums":{"topic":"enums","title":"Enums","quiz":[{"q":"What does an enum represent?","options":["Several values all present together","A value that is exactly one of several named variants","A list that can grow","A number with units"],"answer":1,"explain":"An enum says a value is exactly one of its variants — like a traffic light being red OR yellow OR green, never several at once."},{"q":"Which is true about enum variants in Rust?","options":["Variants can never hold data","All variants must hold the same type of data","Each variant can hold its own, different shape of data (or none)","Variants are just numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust enum variants can each carry different data: nothing, a tuple, or named fields. That's what makes them powerful for modeling."},{"q":"What is `Option` in Rust?","options":["A settings menu","A built-in enum with Some(value) and None, used instead of null","A kind of struct","A loop keyword"],"answer":1,"explain":"Option is an enum: `Some(value)` means there's a value, `None` means there isn't. It replaces null and forces you to handle the missing case."},{"q":"How do you refer to the `North` variant of an enum named `Direction`?","options":["North","Direction.North","Direction::North","North::Direction"],"answer":2,"explain":"You select a variant with `EnumName::Variant`, so `Direction::North`, using the `::` path syntax."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does an enum model?","back":"A value that is exactly ONE of several named variants — 'one of these', not 'all of these together' (that's a struct)."},{"front":"Can enum variants hold data?","back":"Yes, and each variant can hold a different shape: nothing (Quit), a tuple (Write(String)), or named fields (Move { x, y })."},{"front":"What is Option and why does Rust use it?","back":"An enum with Some(value) and None. It replaces null: 'might be missing' is in the type, so you're forced to handle the None case."},{"front":"How do you pick an enum variant?","back":"With `EnumName::Variant`, e.g. `Direction::North` — the `::` path syntax."}]},"pattern-matching":{"topic":"pattern-matching","title":"Pattern matching","quiz":[{"q":"What does it mean that a `match` must be exhaustive?","options":["It must have exactly one arm","It must cover every possible value — handle all cases or add `_`","It must be slow and thorough","It can only match numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust requires a match to handle every possible value. Miss a case and it won't compile — use `_` as a catch-all for the rest."},{"q":"In the arm `Some(n) => ...`, what is `n`?","options":["A comparison against an existing variable n","A new name that captures the value inside Some (destructuring)","The number of the arm","Always zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"`n` is a fresh name that binds the value held inside Some. This is destructuring — the pattern branches and extracts the inner data."},{"q":"When is `if let` a better choice than `match`?","options":["When you need to handle every variant","When you only care about one variant and want to ignore the rest","Never; it's deprecated","Only inside loops"],"answer":1,"explain":"`if let` is the compact form for handling a single pattern. A full match with a throwaway `_ => ()` is clunkier for that case."},{"q":"Where should the catch-all `_` arm go in a match?","options":["First, so it's checked first","Last, because arms are checked top to bottom","It doesn't matter","In the middle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arms are checked top to bottom. A `_` placed early swallows everything below it, making later arms unreachable. Keep it last."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does 'exhaustive match' mean?","back":"A match must cover every possible value. Handle all cases, or add `_` as a catch-all — otherwise it won't compile."},{"front":"What does destructuring do in a match arm?","back":"It mirrors the shape of the data and binds the inner pieces to new names, e.g. `Some(n)` or `Move { x, y }`. Branch and extract in one move."},{"front":"When to use `if let` instead of `match`?","back":"When you only care about one variant and want to ignore the rest — it's the compact single-pattern form."},{"front":"Where do patterns work besides `match`?","back":"In `let` bindings (`let (a, b) = pair`), function arguments, and `if let`. Same 'shapes with names' idea everywhere."}]},"methods-and-impls":{"topic":"methods-and-impls","title":"Methods and impl blocks","quiz":[{"q":"Which receiver should a method use if it only reads the data?","options":["self","&self","&mut self","no receiver"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&self` borrows the value immutably — perfect for reading. Most methods use it."},{"q":"A method needs to change a field. Which receiver does it need?","options":["&self","self","&mut self","static self"],"answer":2,"explain":"`&mut self` borrows mutably, allowing the method to modify fields. Using `&self` would error with 'cannot assign ... behind a & reference'."},{"q":"What is an associated function?","options":["A method that takes &mut self","A function in an impl block with no `self`, often a constructor, called with `Type::func()`","A function outside any impl","A function that returns nothing"],"answer":1,"explain":"An associated function has no self receiver and is called with `::`, e.g. `String::from(...)` or `Rectangle::new(...)`. Constructors are the common case."},{"q":"How do you call a method (with self) versus an associated function (no self)?","options":["Both use a dot","Both use ::","Method uses a dot (value.method()); associated function uses :: (Type::func())","Method uses ::; associated function uses a dot"],"answer":2,"explain":"Methods with a self receiver use a dot: `rect.area()`. Associated functions (no self) use `::`: `Rectangle::new(...)`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"The three method receivers and their meaning?","back":"&self = borrow to read (most common). &mut self = borrow to change a field. self = consume/take ownership (rare)."},{"front":"To call a `&mut self` method, what must the variable be?","back":"`mut`. e.g. `let mut c = ...;` then `c.increment();`. Otherwise the mutable borrow is rejected."},{"front":"What is an associated function?","back":"A function in an impl with no `self`, called with `Type::func()`. Often a constructor like `new`. `String::from` is a familiar example."},{"front":"Dot vs :: for calling?","back":"value.method() (has self) uses a dot. Type::func() (associated function, no self) uses ::."}]},"ownership":{"topic":"ownership","title":"Ownership","quiz":[{"q":"After `let s2 = s1;` where s1 is a String, what happens to s1?","options":["s1 still holds a copy of the string","s1 is moved from and can no longer be used","s1 and s2 share the string and both stay valid","s1 is set to an empty string \"\""],"answer":1,"explain":"For an owning type like String, assignment moves ownership. s1 no longer owns anything, so using it is a compile error."},{"q":"Why can you still use `x` after `let y = x;` when x is an integer?","options":["Integers are special-cased by the compiler","Integers are Copy types, so x is copied, not moved","It's actually a bug and will warn you","Because y is a reference to x"],"answer":1,"explain":"Small stack values like integers implement Copy, so they are duplicated instead of moved. Both x and y remain valid."},{"q":"When is a value's memory freed in Rust?","options":["When a garbage collector decides to run","When you call free() yourself","When its owner goes out of scope","Never, until the program exits"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust drops a value (frees its memory) automatically when the owning variable goes out of scope — no GC, no manual free."},{"q":"You passed a String into a function and now the compiler says it was 'moved'. What's the cheapest correct fix if you still need it afterward?","options":["Call .clone() when passing it, or borrow it with &","Declare the variable twice","Add the `mut` keyword","Wrap it in println!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passing by value moves the String. Clone makes a separate copy; borrowing with & lends it without moving (and is cheaper)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"State the three ownership rules.","back":"1) Each value has one owner. 2) Only one owner at a time. 3) When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped (freed)."},{"front":"What does 'move' mean in Rust?","back":"Assigning or passing an owning value (String, Vec, ...) transfers ownership to the new place. The original variable can no longer be used."},{"front":"Which types are copied instead of moved?","back":"Copy types: simple fixed-size stack values like integers, bool, and char. `let y = x;` copies them, so x stays usable."},{"front":"How do you use a value without giving up ownership?","back":"Borrow it with a reference (&). That lends access without moving, so the original owner keeps the value. (See the Borrowing lesson.)"}]},"borrowing":{"topic":"borrowing","title":"Borrowing","quiz":[{"q":"What does passing `&s` (instead of `s`) to a function do?","options":["Copies the whole String so the function gets its own","Lends a reference to s without giving up ownership","Moves s into the function permanently","Deletes s after the function runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&s` borrows: the function gets a reference and can use the value, but ownership stays with the caller, so s is still usable afterward."},{"q":"You have a read-only borrow `&String` and try to call `.push('!')` on it. What happens?","options":["It works and changes the string","It compiles but does nothing at runtime","A compile error: you can't mutate through a `&` borrow","It makes a copy and mutates that"],"answer":2,"explain":"A plain `&` borrow is read-only. To change the value you need a mutable borrow, `&mut`, and the original variable must be declared `mut`."},{"q":"At one moment, for a single value, which combination of borrows is allowed?","options":["One `&mut` and two `&` at the same time","Two `&mut` at the same time","Either many `&` readers OR one `&mut` writer, never both","Any number of `&mut` writers"],"answer":2,"explain":"The borrowing rule: you may have many read-only borrows, or exactly one mutable borrow, but never a mutable borrow alongside any other borrow."},{"q":"What three things must line up to mutate a value through a function?","options":["The variable is `mut`, you pass `&mut`, the parameter is `&mut`","The variable is `const`, you pass `&`, the parameter is `String`","You call .clone(), pass by value, and return it","Nothing special; any `&` reference can mutate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mutable borrowing needs all three: the original declared `mut`, the value passed as `&mut`, and the parameter typed `&mut`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is borrowing?","back":"Using a value through a reference (&) without taking ownership. The function accesses the value and the original owner keeps it."},{"front":"Difference between `&` and `&mut`?","back":"`&` is a read-only borrow (look, don't change). `&mut` is a mutable borrow (allowed to change the value), and the original must be `mut`."},{"front":"State the core borrowing rule.","back":"At any one time you can have many immutable (&) borrows OR exactly one mutable (&mut) borrow of a value — never both together."},{"front":"Why prefer borrowing over .clone()?","back":"Borrowing moves and copies nothing — it's free. Cloning duplicates the whole value every time. Reach for & before reaching for .clone()."}]},"references-and-dereference":{"topic":"references-and-dereference","title":"References and dereference","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `*` operator do?","options":["Creates a new reference to a value","Follows a reference to reach the value it points at","Multiplies two references together","Frees the memory a reference points at"],"answer":1,"explain":"`*` dereferences: it follows the reference (signpost) back to the actual value. `&` makes a reference; `*` follows one — they are opposites."},{"q":"Given `let r = &x;` where x is an i32, why does `r + 1` fail but `*r + 1` work?","options":["`r` is a reference, not a number; `*r` gets the number so + works","`r + 1` works fine, the question is wrong","You must always write `&` before arithmetic","`*r` copies x into a new variable named r"],"answer":0,"explain":"`r` is a `&i32` (a signpost), and you can't add 1 to a signpost. `*r` follows it to the actual number, then + 1 works."},{"q":"Why can you call `r.len()` without writing `*` when r is a `&String`?","options":["String has a special no-deref rule","The dot operator auto-follows references (deref coercion)","len() secretly takes a reference to a reference","It doesn't work; you must write (*r).len()"],"answer":1,"explain":"The `.` operator automatically follows references as needed to find the method, so method calls rarely need an explicit `*`."},{"q":"How do you add 1 to the value that a `&mut` reference `r` points at?","options":["`r = r + 1;`","`r += 1;`","`*r += 1;`","`&r += 1;`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`*r` follows the mutable reference to the real value, and `+= 1` changes it there. Without `*`, you'd try to change the signpost itself."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a reference, in one image?","back":"A signpost: it points at a value stored elsewhere. It doesn't contain the value itself. You make one with `&`."},{"front":"What does `*` (dereference) do?","back":"Follows a reference back to the value it points at. `&` creates a reference; `*` follows it. They're opposites."},{"front":"When do you need `*` and when not?","back":"Need it for arithmetic and assignment through a reference (`*r + 1`, `*r = 5`). Not for method calls — the dot operator auto-follows references."},{"front":"What is a `&str` compared to `&String`?","back":"A `&str` is a lightweight borrowed view of text. Thanks to deref coercion, a `&String` can be used where a `&str` is expected."}]},"slices":{"topic":"slices","title":"Slices","quiz":[{"q":"What does the slice `&s[0..2]` include?","options":["Indices 0, 1, and 2","Indices 0 and 1 (the end is exclusive)","Just index 2","Indices 1 and 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ranges are start-inclusive, end-exclusive. `0..2` covers indices 0 and 1 and stops before 2 — the first two elements."},{"q":"What kind of thing is a slice?","options":["A brand-new copy of part of the collection","A borrowed view into part of a collection — it owns nothing","A separate owned String","A number counting the elements"],"answer":1,"explain":"A slice is a reference (a borrow) that views part of an existing collection. It copies nothing and owns nothing, so it can't outlive what it borrows."},{"q":"What is a `&str`?","options":["A string slice — a borrowed view into string text","An owned, growable string on the heap","A single character","A vector of bytes you own"],"answer":0,"explain":"A `&str` is a string slice: a borrowed window into text. Both text literals and slices of a String have this type."},{"q":"You write `let piece = word[0..3];` (no `&`) on a String. Why won't it compile?","options":["The range is invalid","A slice is a borrow, so it needs the `&`","You must use () not []","Strings can't be sliced at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"A slice is a reference, so it needs `&`: `&word[0..3]`. Without it you'd be trying to move a chunk out by value, which isn't allowed."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a slice?","back":"A borrowed view into part of a collection (a string or array). It copies nothing, owns nothing, and can't outlive the value it borrows from."},{"front":"How do you write a slice, and what does the range mean?","back":"`&thing[start..end]`. The range is start-inclusive and end-exclusive, so `[0..2]` is the first two elements (0 and 1)."},{"front":"What is a `&str`?","back":"A string slice — a borrowed window into text. Slicing a String gives a &str, and text literals like \"hi\" are also &str."},{"front":"What's a slice of an array called?","back":"A `&[T]` — a borrowed view over a run of elements of type T. Prefer slice parameters (&[T], &str) for read-only access."}]},"lifetimes":{"topic":"lifetimes","title":"Lifetimes","quiz":[{"q":"What does a lifetime actually track?","options":["How much memory a value uses","How long a reference stays valid (how long its target exists)","How many times a value is cloned","The speed of the program"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifetime describes how long a reference is valid, so the compiler can forbid references that would outlive the data they point at."},{"q":"Does adding a lifetime annotation like `'a` make data live longer?","options":["Yes, it extends how long the value exists","No, it only describes a relationship the compiler then checks","Yes, it allocates extra memory","Only for String, not for other types"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifetime annotation describes an existing relationship between references; it never allocates or prolongs anything."},{"q":"When do you usually have to write an explicit lifetime like `'a`?","options":["On every function that uses references","When a function returns a reference and takes more than one reference","Only inside main()","Never — Rust always infers them"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's elision rules cover the common cases. You mainly need explicit lifetimes when a function returns a reference and takes multiple references, so Rust can't tell which the output borrows from."},{"q":"Why does this fail to compile: a reference `r` set to `&value` inside a block, then used after the block ends?","options":["value is dropped at the block's end, so r would dangle","You can't use & inside a block","r needs to be declared mut","Blocks aren't allowed in main()"],"answer":0,"explain":"The value is dropped when its block ends, so r would point at freed memory. The compiler says 'value does not live long enough' and rejects it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a lifetime?","back":"Rust's tracking of how long a reference stays valid — how long the thing it points at is guaranteed to exist. Used to forbid dangling references."},{"front":"Does `'a` make data live longer?","back":"No. A lifetime annotation only describes a relationship between references so the compiler can check it. It never allocates or prolongs anything."},{"front":"When do you need to write explicit lifetimes?","back":"Mostly when a function returns a reference and takes more than one reference, so Rust can't infer which input the output borrows from."},{"front":"How do you read `'a`?","back":"\"tick-a\" — it's a made-up label for a duration (how long), written in <...>, not a type like i32."}]},"traits":{"topic":"traits","title":"Traits","quiz":[{"q":"What is a trait in Rust?","options":["A list of behavior (method signatures) a type promises to provide","A parent class that other types inherit fields from","A special kind of variable that holds many values","A macro that generates code at compile time"],"answer":0,"explain":"A trait describes behavior — a set of methods a type must provide. It's a capability, not a class with fields."},{"q":"You defined `trait Greet` and a `Dog` struct, but `dog.hello()` gives 'no method named hello'. What's missing?","options":["You need to make Dog mutable with `mut`","You need an `impl Greet for Dog { ... }` block","You need to add `#[derive(Greet)]`","Traits can't be used with structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Defining the trait only states the promise. You must write `impl Greet for Dog` to say how Dog actually keeps it."},{"q":"What does the parameter type `&impl Greet` mean?","options":["A reference to the Greet trait itself","A reference to some type that implements Greet","A brand-new Greet value created inline","Only the Dog type is allowed"],"answer":1,"explain":"`&impl Greet` accepts a reference to any type that implements Greet, so one function works for all such types."},{"q":"What is a default method in a trait?","options":["A method that can only be called once","A method every type must override","A method with a body in the trait, so types get it for free unless they override it","A method that runs automatically at program start"],"answer":2,"explain":"A default method provides a body inside the trait. Implementing types inherit it automatically and may override it if they want."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a trait?","back":"A named list of method signatures — a promise of behavior that a type provides by implementing it. Think 'can do', not 'is a'."},{"front":"How do you make a type keep a trait's promise?","back":"Write `impl TraitName for TypeName { ... }` and provide the required methods."},{"front":"How do you write one function that accepts any type implementing a trait?","back":"Use a trait bound, e.g. `fn f(x: &impl Greet)` or `fn f<T: Greet>(x: &T)`."},{"front":"Are traits the same as class inheritance?","back":"No. Traits carry behavior, not fields, and a type can implement many unrelated traits. They express 'can do', not 'is a kind of'."}]},"generics":{"topic":"generics","title":"Generics","quiz":[{"q":"In `fn first<T>(items: &[T]) -> &T`, what is `T`?","options":["A specific type named T defined elsewhere","A placeholder for a type, chosen when the function is called","A variable holding a runtime value","The name of a trait"],"answer":1,"explain":"T is a generic type parameter — a placeholder. The compiler fills it with a concrete type each time you call the function."},{"q":"Why does a generic function often need a trait bound like `<T: PartialOrd>`?","options":["To make the code run faster at runtime","Because T could be any type, so you may only use abilities you promise it has","Bounds are optional decoration with no effect","To turn the generic into a trait object"],"answer":1,"explain":"Inside a generic, T might be anything, so the compiler only allows operations you guarantee via bounds. `PartialOrd` promises T can be compared with `>`."},{"q":"What does the `+` mean in `<T: PartialOrd + Copy>`?","options":["T is either PartialOrd or Copy","Add PartialOrd and Copy together numerically","T must implement both PartialOrd and Copy","It's a syntax error"],"answer":2,"explain":"The `+` combines bounds: T must satisfy PartialOrd AND Copy."},{"q":"Why are Rust generics called 'zero-cost'?","options":["They are free to type on the keyboard","The compiler generates specialized code per concrete type, so there's no runtime penalty","They use a garbage collector to manage memory","They only work with numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"For each concrete type used, the compiler stamps out a specialized version (monomorphization), so generic code runs as fast as hand-written specific code."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a generic type parameter like T?","back":"A placeholder for a type, chosen when the code is used. Declared in angle brackets: fn name<T>(...) or struct Name<T>."},{"front":"Why do you add trait bounds to a generic?","back":"Inside a generic, T could be any type, so you may only use abilities you promise it has. Bounds like <T: PartialOrd> grant those abilities."},{"front":"What does <T: PartialOrd + Copy> mean?","back":"T must implement both PartialOrd (comparable) and Copy (cheaply duplicated). The + means 'and also'."},{"front":"Do generics slow down your program?","back":"No — they are zero-cost. The compiler generates a specialized version per concrete type, so runtime speed matches hand-written code."}]},"collections":{"topic":"collections","title":"Collections","quiz":[{"q":"What is a `Vec<T>`?","options":["A fixed-size array decided at compile time","A growable, ordered list of values of the same type","A lookup table that finds values by key","A borrowed view into text"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec<T> is a growable, ordered list. You add to the end with .push() and index from 0."},{"q":"What happens with `v[99]` when the vector only has 3 items?","options":["It returns None","It returns 0","It panics at runtime with 'index out of bounds'","It grows the vector to 100 items"],"answer":2,"explain":"Indexing out of bounds panics. Use v.get(99), which returns an Option, when the index might not exist."},{"q":"How do you find a value in a `HashMap` by its key?","options":["By its numeric position, like map[0]","With .get(key), which returns an Option","You can't look up individual keys","By looping until you reach the right index"],"answer":1,"explain":"A HashMap is keyed, not positional. .get(key) returns Some(value) if the key exists, or None if it doesn't."},{"q":"Why does `let scores = Vec::new(); scores.push(10);` fail to compile?","options":["Vec::new() is not a real function","push needs the vector to be declared `let mut`","You must specify the type as Vec<i32>","push only works on HashMap"],"answer":1,"explain":"push modifies the vector, so it must be mutable: `let mut scores = Vec::new();`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a Vec<T> and how do you add to it?","back":"A growable, ordered list of same-typed values. Add to the end with .push(); index from 0. Must be `let mut` to modify."},{"front":"How do you safely read an index that might not exist?","back":"Use .get(i), which returns Option (Some or None). Plain v[i] panics if the index is out of bounds."},{"front":"What is a HashMap<K, V> for?","back":"Looking up values by a key you choose. Add pairs with .insert(key, value); read with .get(key). Remember `use std::collections::HashMap;`."},{"front":"How do you print a whole Vec or HashMap?","back":"Use the debug placeholder {:?}, e.g. println!(\"{:?}\", v). Plain {} only works for single simple values."}]},"strings-and-str":{"topic":"strings-and-str","title":"Strings and `str`","quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between `String` and `&str`?","options":["String owns growable text; &str is a borrowed view of existing text","They are identical and interchangeable","&str owns text; String only borrows it","String is for numbers; &str is for letters"],"answer":0,"explain":"String owns a growable heap buffer. &str is a borrowed view into text that lives somewhere else."},{"q":"What type is a string literal like `\"hello\"`?","options":["String","&str","char","Vec<char>"],"answer":1,"explain":"String literals are &str — borrowed views pointing into your compiled program."},{"q":"For a function that only reads text, which parameter type is most flexible?","options":["String, because it owns the data","&str, because both String (via &) and literals can be passed","char, because it's smaller","It doesn't matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"Taking &str lets callers pass a borrowed String or a literal without giving up ownership. Take &str to read, return String to build new text."},{"q":"What does `.len()` return for the string `\"café\"`?","options":["4, the number of characters","5, the number of bytes (é takes 2 bytes in UTF-8)","1, because it's one word","It causes a compile error"],"answer":1,"explain":".len() counts bytes, not characters. 'é' is 2 bytes in UTF-8, so \"café\" is 5 bytes. Use .chars().count() for the character count."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"String vs &str?","back":"String owns growable, heap-allocated text. &str is a borrowed view of text that already exists. String literals are &str."},{"front":"Which text type should a read-only function parameter use?","back":"&str. It accepts both a String (borrowed with &) and a literal, without taking ownership."},{"front":"Why can't you write `some_string[0]` to get a character?","back":"Rust text is UTF-8; byte positions and character positions can differ, so indexing by number is disallowed. Use .chars().nth(i)."},{"front":"Does .len() give the character count?","back":"No — it gives the byte count. Use .chars().count() for the number of characters."}]},"iterators":{"topic":"iterators","title":"Iterators","quiz":[{"q":"What does it mean that iterator adapters like `map` and `filter` are 'lazy'?","options":["They run slowly","They only describe work and do nothing until a consumer runs the chain","They skip every other item","They cache results forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adapters build a plan but touch no items until a consumer (collect, sum, for, ...) pulls values through."},{"q":"Which of these actually runs an iterator chain?","options":[".map(|x| x * 2)",".filter(|x| *x > 0)",".collect()",".rev()"],"answer":2,"explain":"collect is a consumer — it drives the chain and gathers results. map, filter, and rev are lazy adapters that only describe work."},{"q":"What is the difference between `.iter()` and `.into_iter()` on a Vec?","options":["iter() borrows each item; into_iter() takes ownership and consumes the Vec","They are the same","iter() is faster but less safe","into_iter() only works on numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"iter() yields &T (borrows), leaving the Vec usable. into_iter() yields owned T and consumes the collection."},{"q":"Why does `let v = nums.iter().map(|n| n * 2);` (with no consumer) not double anything?","options":["map is broken","Adapters are lazy — without a consumer, no work runs","You must use a for loop instead of map","nums was empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"map is a lazy adapter; it just builds a plan. Add a consumer like .collect() or .sum() to actually run it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does 'iterators are lazy' mean?","back":"Adapters (map, filter) only describe work. Nothing runs until a consumer (collect, sum, count, for) pulls items through the chain."},{"front":"Name some iterator consumers.","back":"collect (into a collection), sum, count, for loops. They finish the chain and produce a value or side effect."},{"front":"iter() vs iter_mut() vs into_iter()?","back":"iter() borrows (&T), iter_mut() borrows mutably (&mut T) to edit in place, into_iter() takes ownership (T) and consumes the collection."},{"front":"Why does collect() sometimes need a type annotation?","back":"collect can build many kinds of collection, so the compiler needs to know which: e.g. `let v: Vec<i32> = it.collect();`."}]},"closures":{"topic":"closures","title":"Closures","quiz":[{"q":"How do you write a closure that takes one parameter `x` and returns `x * 2`?","options":["(x) => x * 2","|x| x * 2","fn(x) x * 2","{x} x * 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Closure parameters go between pipes: |x| x * 2. A single-expression body needs no braces or return."},{"q":"What can a closure do that a normal top-level `fn` cannot?","options":["Return a value","Take parameters","Capture variables from the surrounding scope","Be called more than once"],"answer":2,"explain":"A closure can capture and use variables from the code around it. A plain fn only sees its own parameters."},{"q":"What does the `move` keyword do in `move || println!(\"{}\", name)`?","options":["Runs the closure immediately","Makes the closure take ownership of the variables it captures","Moves the closure to another thread automatically","Prevents the closure from capturing anything"],"answer":1,"explain":"move forces the closure to own its captured values instead of borrowing them — needed when the closure outlives the current scope, like in a thread."},{"q":"By default, how does a closure capture a variable it uses?","options":["By taking ownership (move)","By borrowing it","By copying the whole program","It doesn't capture unless you use `capture`"],"answer":1,"explain":"By default a closure borrows what it captures, so the original variable stays usable. Add move to take ownership."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a closure and how is it written?","back":"An unnamed inline function: |params| body. A one-expression body needs no braces or return, e.g. |x| x + 1."},{"front":"What is a closure's superpower over a plain fn?","back":"It can capture variables from the surrounding scope and use them without passing them in as arguments."},{"front":"How does a closure capture by default, and how do you change it?","back":"It borrows captured variables by default. Add the `move` keyword to make it take ownership (needed when it outlives the scope, e.g. threads)."},{"front":"How does a function accept a closure as a parameter?","back":"Via the Fn / FnMut / FnOnce trait bounds, e.g. fn apply<F: Fn(i32) -> i32>(f: F) { ... }."}]},"error-handling":{"topic":"error-handling","title":"Error handling","quiz":[{"q":"What does `Option<T>` represent?","options":["An operation that failed with a reason","A value that might be present (Some) or absent (None)","A list of many values","A number that can be negative"],"answer":1,"explain":"Option<T> is Some(value) or None — a value that might be missing. It's Rust's safe replacement for null."},{"q":"What are the two shapes of `Result<T, E>`?","options":["Some and None","True and False","Ok(value) and Err(error)","Yes and No"],"answer":2,"explain":"Result is Ok(value) on success or Err(error) on failure, where the Err carries a reason it failed."},{"q":"What does the `?` operator do after a `Result`-returning call?","options":["Always crashes the program on any value","If Ok, gives the inner value; if Err, returns that error early from the function","Prints the error and continues","Converts the Result into a String"],"answer":1,"explain":"? unwraps the Ok value, or short-circuits and returns the Err from the current function. It only works in a function that returns Result or Option."},{"q":"What is the risk of using `unwrap()` in real code?","options":["It's slower than match","It panics (crashes the program) if the value is None or Err","It changes the value's type","It silently ignores errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"unwrap returns the inner value but panics on None/Err, crashing the program. Prefer match, if let, or ? when failure should be handled gracefully."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Option vs Result?","back":"Option<T> = Some(v) or None — a value might be missing. Result<T, E> = Ok(v) or Err(e) — an operation might fail with a reason."},{"front":"What does the ? operator do?","back":"Unwraps an Ok/Some value, or returns the Err/None early from the current function. Only usable in a function that returns Result or Option."},{"front":"When should you avoid unwrap()/expect()?","back":"In real code where failure should be handled — they panic (crash) on None/Err. They're fine for tests and quick scripts."},{"front":"In a `-> Result<i32, E>` function, how do you return a success value?","back":"Wrap it: return Ok(value). A bare value is a type mismatch because the function must return a Result."}]},"testing":{"topic":"testing","title":"Testing","quiz":[{"q":"How do you mark a function as a test that `cargo test` will run?","options":["Put the word `test` in its name","Add the `#[test]` attribute above it","Return a bool from the function","Place it inside fn main()"],"answer":1,"explain":"The `#[test]` attribute is the sticker that tells Cargo to run this function during `cargo test`. Without it, the function is ignored by the test runner."},{"q":"What does `assert_eq!(a, b)` do?","options":["Prints a and b side by side","Sets a equal to b","Fails (panics) the test if a is not equal to b","Returns true or false without stopping the test"],"answer":2,"explain":"`assert_eq!` panics (marking the test failed) when the two values are not equal, and does nothing when they are. It's the workhorse assertion."},{"q":"Why put `#[cfg(test)]` on your test module?","options":["It makes the tests run faster","It compiles the test code only during `cargo test`, not into the real build","It is required or the tests won't be found","It turns off the borrow checker for tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"`#[cfg(test)]` means the module is only compiled when testing, so your test-only code and imports never bloat the shipped program."},{"q":"Where do integration tests live in a Cargo project?","options":["Inside fn main()","In a top-level `tests/` directory","In the `Cargo.toml` file","They must be in the same function as the code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integration tests go in a top-level `tests/` folder; each file there is compiled as its own crate that uses your public API from the outside, like a real user."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you run all tests in a Cargo project?","back":"`cargo test` — it finds every `#[test]` function, runs them, and reports pass/fail."},{"front":"Unit tests vs integration tests?","back":"Unit tests live next to the code (in a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` block) and can see private items. Integration tests live in the `tests/` folder and only see the public API."},{"front":"What happens to a `#[test]` function with no assertions?","back":"It always passes — it's checking nothing. Every test needs at least one assert to be meaningful."},{"front":"What does `#[should_panic]` do?","back":"Marks a test that passes only if the code inside panics — used when panicking is the correct behavior."}]},"file-io":{"topic":"file-io","title":"File I/O","quiz":[{"q":"Why do almost all `std::fs` functions return a `Result`?","options":["Because Rust dislikes files","Because file access can always fail (missing file, no permission, full disk)","So they run faster","To make the code longer on purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"The filesystem is the unreliable outside world. Any read or write can fail, so the API returns a Result and forces you to handle the failure case."},{"q":"What does the `?` operator do on a line like `let t = fs::read_to_string(p)?;`?","options":["Prints the error and continues","Ignores any error silently","Unwraps the Ok value, or returns the Err from the current function","Retries the read three times"],"answer":2,"explain":"`?` gives you the value on success, or immediately returns the error to the caller on failure — but only inside a function that itself returns Result or Option."},{"q":"What does `fs::write(\"log.txt\", data)` do if the file already exists?","options":["Appends the data to the end","Overwrites the file completely, erasing the old contents","Returns an error and does nothing","Creates log.txt.bak first"],"answer":1,"explain":"`fs::write` replaces the entire file. To add to an existing file instead, open it with `OpenOptions::new().append(true)`."},{"q":"Which requirement must a function meet to use the `?` operator inside it?","options":["It must be named main","It must return `Result` (or `Option`)","It must be marked `unsafe`","It must take no arguments"],"answer":1,"explain":"`?` needs somewhere to send an error, so the enclosing function must return a Result or Option. That's why file-reading helpers usually return `Result<String, io::Error>`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Read an entire text file into a String?","back":"`std::fs::read_to_string(path)` — returns a `Result<String, io::Error>`."},{"front":"Write a string to a file (creating/overwriting it)?","back":"`std::fs::write(path, data)` — returns a Result; it overwrites any existing file."},{"front":"Add to a file without erasing it?","back":"Open it with `OpenOptions::new().create(true).append(true).open(path)`, then write to it."},{"front":"Why prefer `?` over `.unwrap()` in real programs?","back":"`.unwrap()`/`.expect()` crash the whole program on the first error; `?` propagates the error so it can be handled gracefully."}]},"concurrency":{"topic":"concurrency","title":"Concurrency","quiz":[{"q":"What does `handle.join()` do after `thread::spawn`?","options":["Starts a second thread","Waits (pauses) until that thread finishes","Cancels the thread immediately","Merges two threads into one core"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.join()` blocks the current thread until the spawned thread completes. Without it, main might end and cut the worker off mid-task."},{"q":"Why do you often need `move` in `thread::spawn(move || { ... })`?","options":["It makes the thread run faster","It gives the thread ownership of the values it uses, so they can't be dropped out from under it","It is just decoration","It moves the thread to another CPU core"],"answer":1,"explain":"`move` transfers ownership of captured values into the thread. Otherwise the compiler can't prove the borrowed data outlives the thread."},{"q":"You need several threads to co-own and mutate one counter safely. What do you reach for?","options":["`Rc<RefCell<i32>>`","`Arc<Mutex<i32>>`","A plain `&mut i32` shared around","`Box<i32>`"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Arc` provides thread-safe shared ownership and `Mutex` provides one-at-a-time mutation. `Rc`/`RefCell` are single-threaded and won't compile across threads."},{"q":"What do the standard channel functions in `std::sync::mpsc` give you?","options":["A shared mutable global variable","A pipe: a sender (tx) and receiver (rx) for passing messages between threads","A way to disable the borrow checker","Automatic parallelism with no threads"],"answer":1,"explain":"`mpsc::channel()` returns a (tx, rx) pair. Threads communicate by sending messages down the pipe rather than sharing memory directly — the safest sharing style."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Start work on a new thread?","back":"`thread::spawn(|| { ... })` returns a JoinHandle; call `.join()` to wait for it."},{"front":"`Rc` vs `Arc`?","back":"`Rc` is shared ownership for a single thread (faster); `Arc` is the thread-safe version for sharing across threads."},{"front":"Safest way for threads to communicate?","back":"Channels (`std::sync::mpsc`) — send messages through a tx/rx pipe instead of sharing mutable memory."},{"front":"What do `Send` and `Sync` mean?","back":"`Send` = safe to move to another thread; `Sync` = safe to share by reference across threads. The compiler checks them automatically."}]},"async-await":{"topic":"async-await","title":"Async and await","quiz":[{"q":"What does calling an `async fn` actually do?","options":["Runs its body immediately and returns the result","Returns a future — inert work that runs nothing until it is awaited or driven","Spawns a new thread","Blocks until the work is done"],"answer":1,"explain":"An async fn returns a future. Its body does not run when you call it; something must `.await` it (or a runtime must drive it) for anything to happen."},{"q":"Where can you use `.await`?","options":["Anywhere, including normal functions","Only inside `async` functions or blocks","Only inside `fn main`","Only inside `unsafe` blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"`.await` is only legal inside async code, because it needs the async state machine around it. In a normal function you hand the future to a runtime instead."},{"q":"Rust the language ships `async`/`.await`. What does it NOT ship?","options":["The `.await` keyword","An executor/runtime to actually drive futures","The ability to define async functions","The `Future` trait"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust provides the syntax and the Future trait but no executor. You pick a runtime like Tokio to drive your top-level future to completion."},{"q":"Which workload is async best suited for?","options":["CPU-heavy number crunching across cores","I/O-bound work with lots of waiting (network, disk), where one thread juggles many tasks","Anything, it's always faster than threads","Only single-step programs with no waiting"],"answer":1,"explain":"Async shines for I/O-bound work: tasks spend most of their time waiting, so one thread can handle thousands. CPU-bound crunching is better served by threads."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does an `async fn` return?","back":"A future — a value representing work that will produce a result later. It runs nothing until awaited or driven by a runtime."},{"front":"What does `.await` do?","back":"Drives a future to completion; if it must wait on I/O it parks the task and lets other tasks run, resuming when ready. Only usable inside async code."},{"front":"What does `#[tokio::main]` do?","back":"Lets `main` be `async`, starts the Tokio runtime, and drives your top-level future — without a runtime, async code never actually runs."},{"front":"Async vs threads?","back":"Async = I/O-bound waiting (many idle tasks on few threads). Threads = CPU-bound work across cores."}]},"macros":{"topic":"macros","title":"Macros","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `!` in `println!` tell you?","options":["It makes the output louder","It marks a macro call, not a regular function call","It means the function can fail","It ends the statement"],"answer":1,"explain":"The `!` distinguishes a macro invocation from a function call. There is no function named `println` — only the macro, which is why dropping the `!` errors."},{"q":"When does a macro do its work?","options":["At runtime, each time it is reached","At compile time — it expands into ordinary Rust code before the program runs","Only during `cargo test`","Never; macros are just comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"A macro is code that writes code. It expands at compile time into normal Rust, which is then compiled. By the time the program runs, only the generated code remains."},{"q":"In a `macro_rules!` pattern, what does `$msg:expr` capture?","options":["A type named msg","One expression, bound to the name `$msg`","A whole file","A string literal only"],"answer":1,"explain":"`$msg:expr` captures a single expression and names it `$msg`. The `:expr` is a fragment specifier telling the macro what kind of code to expect."},{"q":"Which is a good reason to write a macro instead of a function?","options":["You want to accept a variable number of arguments, like println!","You want the code to run faster at runtime","You dislike writing function signatures","You want to skip type checking entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Functions take a fixed number of arguments; macros can accept any number and can generate code. Prefer a plain function unless you need something functions can't do."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a macro, in one line?","back":"Code that writes code — it expands into ordinary Rust at compile time. The `!` (as in `println!`) marks a macro call."},{"front":"What is `macro_rules!`?","back":"The declarative macro system: you pattern-match the input code (`($x:expr) => { ... }`) and produce output code. The kind you'll write most often."},{"front":"What does `$( ... ),*` mean in a macro?","back":"Zero-or-more repetition separated by commas — how a macro accepts a variable-length list, like `vec!` and `println!` do."},{"front":"Declarative vs procedural macros?","back":"Declarative = `macro_rules!` pattern matching (common, easy). Procedural = programs that transform token streams, like `#[derive(Debug)]` (advanced, mostly used not written)."}]},"smart-pointers":{"topic":"smart-pointers","title":"Smart pointers","quiz":[{"q":"What does `Box<T>` give you?","options":["Shared ownership across many owners","A single-owner value stored on the heap","Thread-safe reference counting","Runtime-checked borrowing"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Box<T>` puts one value on the heap with a single owner. It's needed for large values and for recursive types whose size would otherwise be unknown."},{"q":"What does `Rc::clone(&value)` do?","options":["Makes a deep copy of the underlying data","Adds another owner and bumps the reference count (cheap, no data copy)","Moves the value to a new thread","Frees the value"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Rc::clone` is cheap: it creates another handle pointing at the same data and increments the owner count. The data itself is shared, not duplicated."},{"q":"You need several threads to share ownership of one value. Which do you use?","options":["`Rc<T>`","`Box<T>`","`Arc<T>`","A plain reference `&T`"],"answer":2,"explain":"`Arc<T>` is the thread-safe reference-counted pointer. `Rc<T>` isn't thread-safe and won't compile across threads; use `Arc` when threads are involved."},{"q":"Why is `Box` required for a recursive type like a linked-list node?","options":["Because recursion is not allowed in Rust","A Box is pointer-sized, giving the type a finite, known size the compiler can compute","Because Box makes it run faster","It isn't required; recursive types work without any indirection"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without indirection, a node containing itself would have infinite size. A `Box` is a fixed-size pointer, so the compiler can finally compute the type's size."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"`Box<T>` — what and when?","back":"Single owner, value on the heap. Use for large values and recursive types (like linked lists) where the size must be finite and known."},{"front":"`Rc<T>` — what and when?","back":"Multiple owners of one value, single-threaded. `Rc::clone` bumps an owner count cheaply; the value drops when the last owner goes away."},{"front":"`Rc` vs `Arc`?","back":"Same idea; `Arc` uses a thread-safe (atomic) counter so it can be shared across threads. `Rc` is single-threaded and slightly faster."},{"front":"Can you mutate through `Rc`/`Arc`?","back":"Not directly — they give shared read access. Combine with `RefCell` (single-thread) or `Mutex` (multi-thread) to mutate shared data."}]},"interior-mutability":{"topic":"interior-mutability","title":"Interior mutability","quiz":[{"q":"What is interior mutability?","options":["Turning off the borrow checker entirely","Mutating a value through a shared `&` reference, with the borrow rules checked at runtime","A way to make all fields public","Making a struct thread-safe automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Interior mutability lets you change data even when you only hold a shared `&`. The type (like RefCell) enforces the borrow rules itself, at runtime instead of compile time."},{"q":"What happens if you hold a `RefCell` read borrow and call `borrow_mut()` at the same time?","options":["It's a compile error","It silently allows both","It panics at runtime ('already borrowed')","It returns None"],"answer":2,"explain":"RefCell enforces 'many readers or one writer' at runtime. Violating it compiles fine but panics with a BorrowMutError when it runs."},{"q":"Which combination gives shared ownership AND mutation in single-threaded code?","options":["`Box<RefCell<T>>`","`Rc<RefCell<T>>`","`Arc<Mutex<T>>`","`Rc<T>` alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"`Rc` provides multiple owners and `RefCell` provides interior mutability. Together, several owners can each mutate the shared value (single-threaded)."},{"q":"Why can't you use `RefCell` to share mutable state across threads?","options":["It's too slow","It isn't thread-safe (not Sync), so the compiler rejects it; use `Mutex`/`RwLock` instead","It only holds numbers","It requires unsafe code"],"answer":1,"explain":"RefCell's runtime borrow tracking isn't thread-safe, so it can't cross thread boundaries. The thread-safe equivalents are Mutex and RwLock."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does `RefCell<T>` let you do?","back":"Mutate through a shared `&` via `.borrow()` / `.borrow_mut()`, enforcing 'many readers or one writer' at runtime — panicking if you break it."},{"front":"`Cell<T>` vs `RefCell<T>`?","back":"`Cell` is for small `Copy` values via `get`/`set` and never panics; `RefCell` lends references and checks borrows at runtime."},{"front":"The single-thread combo for shared, mutable data?","back":"`Rc<RefCell<T>>` — Rc for multiple owners, RefCell for interior mutation."},{"front":"The multi-thread equivalents?","back":"`Arc` replaces `Rc`, and `Mutex`/`RwLock` replace `RefCell`. So `Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the thread-safe twin of `Rc<RefCell<T>>`."}]},"unsafe-rust":{"topic":"unsafe-rust","title":"Unsafe Rust","quiz":[{"q":"What does the `unsafe` keyword actually do?","options":["Turns off all safety checks for the whole program","Unlocks a small fixed set of extra operations the compiler can't prove safe","Makes the code run faster automatically","Disables the borrow checker everywhere"],"answer":1,"explain":"`unsafe` only unlocks five specific operations (like dereferencing raw pointers and calling unsafe functions). It does NOT disable safety for the rest of your program."},{"q":"Which of these requires an `unsafe` block?","options":["Creating a raw pointer with `&x as *const i32`","Dereferencing a raw pointer with `*p`","Declaring a variable","Calling a normal safe function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Creating a raw pointer is safe (it's just a number). Actually following it with `*p` could read invalid memory, so dereferencing requires `unsafe`."},{"q":"What is the recommended pattern for using unsafe code?","options":["Spread unsafe blocks throughout the codebase for speed","Wrap a small, checked unsafe core inside a safe API so callers never touch unsafe","Mark the whole program `unsafe`","Use unsafe to silence borrow-checker errors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The standard library does this everywhere (e.g. Vec): a safe function checks the conditions, then a tiny unsafe block does the dangerous operation. Callers stay in safe Rust."},{"q":"What happens if you break the contract of an unsafe block (e.g. dereference a dangling pointer)?","options":["A clean compile error","Undefined behavior — crashes, corruption, or silent wrong results","The block is skipped automatically","Rust rolls it back safely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unsafe is a promise you make to the compiler. Break it and you get undefined behavior: the exact crashes and corruption Rust normally prevents. There's no partial credit."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Does `unsafe` disable the borrow checker?","back":"No. It only unlocks five operations (raw-pointer deref, unsafe fn calls, mutable statics, unsafe traits, unions). Ordinary ownership rules still apply."},{"front":"The golden pattern for unsafe code?","back":"Wrap a small, checked `unsafe` core inside a safe API — like `Vec` does — so callers never write `unsafe` themselves."},{"front":"What is undefined behavior?","back":"The result of breaking an unsafe promise (dangling pointer, bad FFI call, uninitialized memory): crashes, corruption, or code that works then fails later."},{"front":"How often should application code use `unsafe`?","back":"Almost never. It's mainly for FFI, low-level data structures, or a measured performance need. Prefer a safe alternative whenever one exists."}]},"ffi":{"topic":"ffi","title":"FFI","quiz":[{"q":"Why must every call to a C function declared in an `extern \"C\"` block be inside `unsafe`?","options":["Because C is an older language than Rust","Because Rust cannot verify the foreign function upholds its safety guarantees","Because `unsafe` makes FFI calls run faster","Because the compiler needs `unsafe` to link the program"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust's safety checks stop at the FFI border. The compiler can't verify what C does, so you take responsibility with `unsafe`."},{"q":"What does `#[repr(C)]` on a struct do?","options":["Makes the struct serialize to JSON","Forces C-compatible field layout so C reads the fields at the right offsets","Marks the struct as unsafe to use","Automatically frees the struct's memory"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without `#[repr(C)]`, Rust may reorder or pad fields differently than C, corrupting data across the boundary. `#[repr(C)]` fixes the layout to match C."},{"q":"Why do you add `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` (or `#[no_mangle]`) when exposing a Rust function to C?","options":["To make the function public","To keep the symbol name unscrambled so C can find it by name","To make the function async","To enable serde serialization"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rust normally mangles function names into long unique symbols. `no_mangle` keeps the plain name so C's linker can find it."},{"q":"Why can't you pass a Rust `String` directly to a C function expecting a C string?","options":["Rust Strings are encrypted","A Rust String isn't zero-terminated and can contain interior nulls","C strings are always longer","Rust Strings can't leave the stack"],"answer":1,"explain":"C strings end in a `\\0` and Rust `String`s don't. You convert with `CString` (Rust->C) and `CStr` (C->Rust)."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does FFI stand for and what is it for?","back":"Foreign Function Interface — calling code in another language (usually C) from Rust, or exposing Rust functions to C."},{"front":"What does `extern \"C\" { ... }` do?","back":"Declares foreign functions (name and signature) so Rust can call them. The \"C\" names the ABI. Calls are always unsafe."},{"front":"What attribute forces C-compatible struct layout, and why is it needed?","back":"`#[repr(C)]`. Without it, Rust may reorder/pad fields differently than C, corrupting data that crosses the boundary."},{"front":"Who frees memory that Rust allocates and hands to C?","back":"There's no borrow checker across FFI — you must decide and document the ownership rule. Freeing on both sides causes double-free crashes."}]},"serde-and-json":{"topic":"serde-and-json","title":"Serde and JSON","quiz":[{"q":"What do the two halves of the name 'Serde' mean?","options":["Server and Debug","Serialize (Rust -> text) and Deserialize (text -> Rust)","Serial and Decode","Secure and Decrypt"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serde = Serialize + Deserialize: turning Rust values into text/bytes and reading them back into Rust types."},{"q":"What does `#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]` above a struct do?","options":["Marks the struct as unsafe","Generates the conversion code between the struct and formats like JSON at compile time","Sends the struct over the network","Makes all fields public"],"answer":1,"explain":"The derive generates all the serialize/deserialize code for you at compile time, based on the struct's fields."},{"q":"Why does `serde_json::from_str` return a `Result`?","options":["Because JSON is always invalid","Because deserializing untrusted input can fail (missing fields, wrong types, malformed text)","Because Result is required for all functions","Because it needs to return two values"],"answer":1,"explain":"Deserializing reads outside data you don't control. Bad input becomes an Err you can handle instead of a crash."},{"q":"The JSON sends a key `displayName` but your Rust field is `display_name`. What fixes the mismatch?","options":["Rename your Rust field to displayName","Add `#[serde(rename = \"displayName\")]` above the field","Nothing — serde ignores case","Wrap the field in Result"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serde matches by field name. `#[serde(rename = ...)]` (or `rename_all`) maps between the JSON key and your Rust field name."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which two crates do you add for JSON with serde, and what feature does serde need?","back":"`serde` (with the `derive` feature) and `serde_json`. e.g. `cargo add serde --features derive` then `cargo add serde_json`."},{"front":"How do you convert a struct to a JSON string and back?","back":"`serde_json::to_string(&value)` -> JSON String; `serde_json::from_str(text)` -> your type. Both return a Result."},{"front":"How do you model a JSON field that may be missing?","back":"Use `Option<T>`. Present -> Some(value), absent -> None, instead of a deserialization error."},{"front":"What does `#[serde(rename = \"...\")]` do?","back":"Maps a JSON key name to a differently-named Rust field, so your Rust can stay snake_case while matching the wire format."}]},"cli-apps":{"topic":"cli-apps","title":"CLI apps","quiz":[{"q":"In `std::env::args()`, what is the first element (`args[0]`)?","options":["The first flag the user passed","The program's own name","Always an empty string","The current directory"],"answer":1,"explain":"args[0] is the program name. The first real user argument is args[1]."},{"q":"Why prefer `args.get(1)` over `args[1]` to read the first argument?","options":["It's faster","`args[1]` panics if the argument is missing, while `.get(1)` returns an Option you can handle","`.get(1)` returns a reference and `args[1]` doesn't","There is no difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"Indexing out of range panics. `.get(1)` gives Some/None so a missing argument becomes a case you handle, not a crash."},{"q":"Where should a CLI print error and status messages?","options":["stdout, using println!","stderr, using eprintln!","A log file only","It doesn't matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"stdout is for the real result (so it can be piped/redirected cleanly); errors and status go to stderr via eprintln!."},{"q":"What exit code should a CLI return when it fails?","options":["0","Any non-zero value","Always 200","It can't set an exit code"],"answer":1,"explain":"0 means success; non-zero means failure. Scripts rely on this, e.g. `myapp && echo ok` only runs if myapp exited 0."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do you read command-line arguments with just the standard library?","back":"`let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();` — args[0] is the program name, real args start at args[1]."},{"front":"stdout vs stderr — what goes where in a CLI?","back":"stdout (println!) = the real result/data. stderr (eprintln!) = status, warnings, errors. Keep them separate so piping works."},{"front":"How does a program report success or failure to the shell?","back":"Its exit code: 0 = success, non-zero = failure. Set it with std::process::exit(code) or return Result from main."},{"front":"When should you use the `clap` crate?","back":"For anything beyond one or two arguments. clap parses flags/options and generates --help, --version, and error messages for you."}]},"logging-and-tracing":{"topic":"logging-and-tracing","title":"Logging and tracing","quiz":[{"q":"You call `tracing::info!` everywhere but nothing prints. What's the most likely cause?","options":["You need to restart your computer","No subscriber is installed — the macros emit events but nobody is listening","info! only works in release builds","You must call info! twice"],"answer":1,"explain":"In tracing, macros only emit events. A separate subscriber (e.g. tracing_subscriber::fmt::init()) must be installed once at startup or nothing prints."},{"q":"Which list shows log levels from least to most severe?","options":["error, warn, info, debug, trace","trace, debug, info, warn, error","info, trace, warn, debug, error","debug, info, warn, error, trace"],"answer":1,"explain":"From noisiest/least severe to most severe: trace, debug, info, warn, error. You set a threshold and everything below it is dropped."},{"q":"Why prefer `info!(user_id = id, \"logged in\")` over `info!(\"user {id} logged in\")`?","options":["It's shorter to type","The value becomes a searchable structured field instead of unsearchable text","String formatting is not allowed in tracing","It changes the log level"],"answer":1,"explain":"Structured fields are labeled key/values that log tools can filter on (e.g. user_id = 7). Values glued into the message are just text."},{"q":"What does a tracing `span` give you that a single `info!` event does not?","options":["Colored output","A duration of work whose context tags every log inside it (e.g. request_id)","Automatic error handling","Faster logging"],"answer":1,"explain":"Events are single points; a span wraps a chunk of work so every log inside it carries shared context, letting you follow one request end to end."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"In the `tracing` crate, what actually decides where log events go?","back":"A subscriber. The info!/warn!/error! macros only emit events; you must install a subscriber (e.g. tracing_subscriber::fmt::init()) once at startup or nothing prints."},{"front":"Name the five log levels from least to most severe.","back":"trace, debug, info, warn, error. Set a threshold; everything below it is dropped."},{"front":"What is a structured field in tracing?","back":"A labeled key/value attached to a log event, e.g. info!(user_id = 7, \"...\"), that log tools can search and filter on — unlike text buried in the message."},{"front":"What is a span, and how does it differ from an event?","back":"A span represents a duration of work; every log inside it is tagged with the span's context (like request_id). Events are single points in time."}]},"docs-and-rustfmt":{"topic":"docs-and-rustfmt","title":"Docs and rustfmt","quiz":[{"q":"Which comment style becomes documentation that `cargo doc` collects?","options":["// two slashes","/// three slashes (a doc comment)","/* block comment */","# hash comment"],"answer":1,"explain":"`///` is a doc comment attached to the item below it, which cargo doc turns into documentation. `//` is a normal comment and is ignored by cargo doc."},{"q":"What does `cargo fmt` do?","options":["Runs your tests","Reformats your code to the standard Rust style automatically","Publishes your crate","Checks for logic bugs"],"answer":1,"explain":"cargo fmt rewrites your code in the official style (spacing, indentation, wrapping) so everyone's code looks the same. It's not about logic."},{"q":"What happens to code examples written inside doc comments when you run `cargo test`?","options":["They are ignored","They actually run as doctests and their assertions are checked","They are deleted","They cause a compile error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Code blocks in doc comments are doctests: cargo test runs them and checks their assertions, so documentation examples can't silently go stale."},{"q":"What does the `//!` comment style document?","options":["The item directly below it","The enclosing module or file it sits inside","Nothing — it's a syntax error","Only test functions"],"answer":1,"explain":"`//!` documents the module/file it's inside (put at the top of a file), while `///` documents the item that follows it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the difference between `//` and `///`?","back":"`//` is a normal comment (ignored by cargo doc). `///` is a doc comment that cargo doc turns into documentation for the item below it."},{"front":"What does `cargo fmt` do, and why use it?","back":"Reformats your whole project to the standard Rust style automatically. It ends style debates and keeps version-control diffs about real changes."},{"front":"What are doctests?","back":"Code examples inside doc comments. `cargo test` runs them and checks their assertions, so docs stay correct."},{"front":"How do you generate and view your project's docs?","back":"`cargo doc --open` builds a browsable HTML site for your crate and dependencies and opens it in your browser."}]},"workspaces-and-crates":{"topic":"workspaces-and-crates","title":"Workspaces and crates","quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a binary crate and a library crate?","options":["A binary crate is faster","A binary crate has a `main` function and runs; a library crate has no `main` and is used by other crates","A library crate can't have dependencies","There is no difference"],"answer":1,"explain":"A binary crate produces a runnable program (has main). A library crate produces code for other crates to use (no main), like serde or axum."},{"q":"What do all crates in a Cargo workspace share?","options":["One main function","One Cargo.lock and one target/ build folder","The same source file","Nothing"],"answer":1,"explain":"A workspace shares a single Cargo.lock (so everyone uses the same dependency versions) and one target/ directory (so dependencies compile once)."},{"q":"What should a pure workspace-root `Cargo.toml` contain?","options":["A `[package]` section describing the program","A `[workspace]` section with a `members` list and (usually) no `[package]`","Only `[dependencies]`","A `main` function"],"answer":1,"explain":"The workspace root coordinates crates; it has `[workspace]` with members and no `[package]`. Each member has its own [package] Cargo.toml."},{"q":"How do you depend on a local crate in the same workspace?","options":["You can't — only crates.io crates work","Point at its folder: `core = { path = \"../core\" }`","Copy its source into your crate","List it under [workspace] only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cargo looks up crates.io by default, so a local crate needs an explicit path. You can then use its `pub` items."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a crate, and what are the two kinds?","back":"A crate is one Rust compile unit. A binary crate has `main` and runs; a library crate has no main and is used by other crates."},{"front":"What is a workspace and what does it share?","back":"A workspace groups multiple crates under one root. They share one Cargo.lock (same dependency versions) and one target/ folder (compile once)."},{"front":"What goes in a pure workspace-root Cargo.toml?","back":"A `[workspace]` section with a `members` list and (usually) no `[package]`. Each member crate has its own [package] Cargo.toml."},{"front":"How do you depend on another crate in the same workspace?","back":"Give it a path: `other = { path = \"../other\" }`. You can only use its `pub` items — the compiler enforces the boundary."}]},"clippy-and-formatting":{"topic":"clippy-and-formatting","title":"Clippy and formatting","quiz":[{"q":"How is clippy different from the compiler?","options":["Clippy is faster than the compiler","The compiler stops code that's wrong; clippy suggests how to improve code that already compiles","Clippy replaces the compiler","Clippy only checks formatting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The compiler answers 'does this work?'. Clippy answers 'is this the idiomatic way?' — it lints code that already compiles fine."},{"q":"Which clippy suggestion is correct?","options":["`x.is_empty()` should become `x.len() == 0`","`x.len() == 0` should become `x.is_empty()`","`for x in &v` should become an index loop","`if flag` should become `if flag == true`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clippy prefers `.is_empty()` over `.len() == 0` — it's clearer and can be faster. The other options are backwards."},{"q":"What jobs do `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` handle, respectively?","options":["Both do the same thing","cargo fmt handles layout/spacing; cargo clippy handles style and logic smells","cargo fmt runs tests; cargo clippy compiles","cargo fmt lints; cargo clippy formats"],"answer":1,"explain":"They don't overlap: cargo fmt makes code look standard (spacing, indentation); cargo clippy makes it read better (idiomatic patterns)."},{"q":"You disagree with one specific clippy lint in one place. What's the right move?","options":["Stop using clippy entirely","Understand why it fired, then silence just that lint with `#[allow(clippy::lint_name)]` if it truly doesn't fit","Delete the code","Ignore all warnings forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Clippy is usually right, but occasionally a lint doesn't fit. Understand it first, then use #[allow(...)] deliberately for that spot only."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does clippy do that the compiler doesn't?","back":"It lints code that already compiles, suggesting more idiomatic/clearer/faster versions — often with the exact fix. The compiler only stops wrong code."},{"front":"What do `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` each handle?","back":"cargo fmt = layout/spacing/indentation (how code looks). cargo clippy = style and logic smells (how code reads). Different, non-overlapping jobs."},{"front":"Give two classic clippy catches.","back":"`x.len() == 0` -> `x.is_empty()`; `if flag == true` -> `if flag`; manual index loop -> `for item in &vec`."},{"front":"How do you silence a single clippy lint you intentionally disagree with?","back":"`#[allow(clippy::lint_name)]` on the item. Do it deliberately after understanding why the lint fired."}]},"web-services":{"topic":"web-services","title":"Web services","quiz":[{"q":"In an Axum web server, what is a handler?","options":["A configuration file","An async function whose return value becomes the HTTP response","A database table","A special kind of macro"],"answer":1,"explain":"A handler is an async fn mapped to a route. You return data (like &str or Json<T>) and the framework turns it into the HTTP response."},{"q":"Why do web handlers use `async`?","options":["To make the code shorter","So a handler can pause on slow work (like a database call) while the server serves other requests","Because Axum requires all functions to be async","To avoid using structs"],"answer":1,"explain":"async lets a handler yield while waiting, so one server can juggle thousands of connections. The Tokio runtime drives this."},{"q":"What must be true of a type `T` for a handler to return `Json<T>`?","options":["T must be an integer","T must implement `Serialize` (usually via `#[derive(Serialize)]`)","T must be async","T must be a String"],"answer":1,"explain":"Json<T> serializes T to JSON, so T must implement Serialize — commonly by adding #[derive(Serialize)] to the struct."},{"q":"Why is doing slow blocking work inside an async handler a problem?","options":["It uses too much disk","It freezes the thread so other requests can't run","It changes the HTTP status code","It's actually fine and recommended"],"answer":1,"explain":"Blocking work stalls the async thread, starving other requests. Use async equivalents or offload heavy work to a dedicated thread."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the shared shape of every web server?","back":"A loop of request in, response out. A route (path + method) maps to a handler function that produces the response."},{"front":"What is an Axum handler, and how does it produce a response?","back":"An `async fn` mapped to a route. Its return value (e.g. &str, String, Json<T>) becomes the HTTP response — the framework serializes it."},{"front":"What starts the async runtime for an Axum server?","back":"`#[tokio::main]` on `async fn main`. Without a runtime, async code never runs."},{"front":"How do handlers share a resource like a database connection pool?","back":"Build it once at startup, attach with `.with_state(state)`, and receive it in handlers via `State<AppState>` — never one connection per request."}]}};
